A={
1:"Aye-aye",
2:"Body 36-44cm, tail 50-60cm, weight about 2-3kg. Lives in northeastern Madagascar. Discovered in the late 18th century, its classification was hotly debated until the mid-19th century, when it was shown to be a primate. It catches food with big ears, rootless front teeth （roots never close, so they grow for life） and a long, thin middle finger. Its famous trick is “tapping”: putting its ear to a tree and knocking on the bark with its middle finger to find grubs hiding inside. Aye-aye babies are also unusually “clingy” for the animal world.",
3:"Aldabra Tortoise",
4:"A land tortoise living on the Aldabra Islands in the Indian Ocean. Its shell can top 120cm and big ones weigh nearly 300kg; with the Galapagos tortoise, it's the world's largest land tortoise. One is recorded living 152 years, and it may live to 200. Caught as food by sailors, it was nearly extinct by the early 20th century, but strict protection has brought it back to 100,000.",
5:"Giant Armadillo",
6:"Body 75-110cm, tail 45-55cm, weight about 45-60kg. Lives east of the Andes, from central Venezuela to northern Argentina. The biggest armadillo, covered in a hard shell. In danger it digs a hole to escape, and when cornered it curls into a ball. Development and road building like the Trans-Amazonian Highway split up its habitat, and now cars are its worst enemy. It's also hunted for food, so its numbers keep falling. It mainly eats termites. By the way, “armadillo” is Spanish for “little armored one.”",
7:"Giant Salamander",
8:"About 60cm from head to tail, sometimes over 120cm. The world's largest amphibian, a “living fossil” found only in limited areas （Honshu west of Gifu, and parts of Shikoku and Kyushu）, and a Special Natural Monument. It has barely evolved in about 30 million years, and may live over 100 years.",
9:"California Condor",
10:"Body about 130cm, wingspan up to 3m: North America's biggest bird. As the name says, it lives in the hills and mountains of central California. Unlike eagles and hawks, its feet are weak, so it can't carry off prey. It doesn't really hunt; it eats dead deer, cows, sheep and such. Eating carcasses before they rot makes it “nature's cleaner.” It declined because people hated it as a carrion bird and hunted it, and because it ate animals killed by poison bait set out for coyotes and ground squirrels. Since 1988, every California condor has been caught and raised by people.",
11:"Yelloweye Penguin",
12:"Body 66-76cm, weight about 4.5-6kg. Lives in southwest New Zealand's South Island, the Auckland Islands, Stewart Island, Campbell Island and more. True to the name its eyes are yellow, with a yellow stripe circling the head from behind the eyes. Unusually for penguins, it raises young in coastal forests. Couples have strong bonds and make happy families. The rarest of all penguins, it's declining as forests become farmland and birds get caught in fishing nets.",
13:"Brown Kiwi",
14:"Large ones reach 80cm, small ones 50cm, weight 2-3kg （females a bit heavier）. A flightless bird found only in New Zealand. It's named for its whistle-like “kiwi！” call. Kiwis are thought to be shrunken relatives of the extinct giant moa, and for their size they lay huge eggs: 12-13cm across and 500g. （6 times a chicken egg, 1/4 of an ostrich egg）",
15:"Barred Bandicoot",
16:"A small bandicoot found only in Western Australia, weighing under 250g. It has stripes on its hips.",
17:"Humboldt Penguin",
18:"Body about 70cm. Lives along the Humboldt Current, its namesake, on South America's Pacific side. It handles heat well for a penguin and is common in zoos, but in the wild its numbers are falling because of people.",
19:"Markhor",
20:"Body 140-165cm, 65-100cm at the shoulder, weight 80-110kg for males and about 32-40kg for females. Lives in northern and central Afghanistan and Pakistan, southern Uzbekistan, Tajikistan, Kashmir and so on. Markhor means “king of wild goats.” It lives in herds in the mountains and runs nimbly over rocks. Males have a long, thick “goatee” and flat, twisted horns up to 140cm.",
21:"Mountain Gorilla",
22:"About 173cm tall and 155kg. Lives in highlands of central Africa. Its size and face made people think it fierce and cruel, but research showed it's strong yet gentle. Grown males are called silverbacks because the hair on their backs turns silver. They usually live in tight groups of 5-10.",
23:"Chinese Alligator",
24:"About 2m from head to tail. As the name says, it lives on the lower Yangtze River in China. It's nocturnal and eats fish, frogs and such. Extremely rare, possibly fewer than 100. People destroying its home and catching it made it decline.",
25:"Rat Snake",
26:"Up to 1.5-2m long, Japan's largest snake; it's not venomous. Found only in Japan. It's gentle and eats bird eggs, mice and such.",
27:"Wood Mouse",
28:"About 10cm from head to tail. Lives in mountains, fields, woods and farms in Hokkaido, Honshu, Shikoku and Kyushu. Its body is reddish brown with white feet and belly, and it eats nuts and such.",
29:"Japanese Mole",
30:"Body about 13cm. Found only in Japan, mainly in the east. Its big, shovel-like hands have 5 large claws, and it lives digging tunnels underground. Bugs often fall into its tunnels, so the mole is said to patrol them and eat whatever it finds.",
31:"Badger",
32:"Body about 40-100cm. Lives in forests in Eurasia and, in Japan, in Honshu, Shikoku and Kyushu. Some places lump it with tanuki as “mujina,” but that's a mix-up, because tanuki fur looks like a badger's and they're often confused.",
33:"Raccoon",
34:"Body about 60cm. Lives in North America. It looks like a tanuki, but its tail has black rings. It lives near forest water and has a habit of washing small animals and fruit before eating. Its cute looks always made it popular, and in Japan it became a star after headlining an anime long ago.",
35:"Impala",
36:"Body 125-160cm, 70-100cm at the shoulder, weight about 53-76kg. Lives in African savanna where acacias grow. Only males have horns, about 45-92cm long. It can jump 2-3m high and 10m far, and run about 90km/h.",
37:"Bullfrog",
38:"A big frog that grows to 15-20cm. Native to North America, it was brought to Japan in the Taisho era and spread. It eats crayfish and bugs, and sometimes other frogs and small fish. It's named for its cow-like call. Apparently it tastes pretty good.",
39:"Grass Lizard",
40:"Grows to about 20cm and lives all over Japan. Though called a “snake” in Japanese, it's a lizard, with a much longer tail than usual. It's quick and eats spiders and such.",
41:"Crabeater Seal",
42:"Body 235cm, weight about 220kg. Lives around Antarctic pack ice. Its whole body is a light gray. Despite the name “crabeater,” it mainly eats krill.",
43:"Hippo",
44:"Body 4m, weight about 2-3 tons. Lives near rivers and swamps south of Africa's Sahara. It's round with a big head and short legs, but swims well and eats plants. It usually grazes on land at night.",
45:"Mantis",
46:"Body about 7-8cm. Lives from Honshu southward. Its sickle-like front legs catch other bugs. It's often said the female eats the male after mating, but that rarely happens in nature. Females see anything moving as food, so more often an unlucky male just gets spotted and eaten.",
47:"Longhorn Beetle",
48:"There are many kinds, over 400 in Japan, the biggest reaching 15cm. Its Japanese name kamikiri means “hair cutter,” because its strong jaws could cleanly snip hair.",
49:"Horseshoe Bat",
50:"Body about 7cm. Lives in North Africa, Eurasia, Japan and so on. The largest insect-eating bat in Japan, it lives in caves. It rarely lives in houses; in Europe people worry it may go extinct.",
51:"Turtle Dove",
52:"Body about 30-35cm. Widespread across Asia. It's not the pigeon you see flocking at shrines and parks （that's the rock dove）. A stylish bird with a grape-colored body, scaly back and a scarf-like pattern on its neck. In Japan it's usually called the mountain dove.",
53:"House Centipede",
54:"Body 2-3cm. Lives from Honshu south, in stone walls near houses, between trees and under fallen leaves. It eats household pests, so it's a “good guy,” but people hate it for looking “gross.”",
55:"Emperor Penguin",
56:"Body 120cm, weight 25-45kg. Lives in Antarctica. The largest penguin, also famous from gum commercials. It makes no nest; the male warms the egg on his feet. Even brief exposure to the air kills the egg, so he can only endure through blizzards. The male eats nothing while warming the egg, and by hatching time he's lost 45% of his weight.",
57:"Sm.-clawed Otter",
58:"Lives on land along rivers and coasts in Southeast Asia, India and southern China. Its small, round claws are adorable and give it its name. On land it scurries about, hopping with its rear held high, but in water it swims sharply using its tail. Its dense fur repels water and dries fast. Superb.",
59:"Striped Skunk",
60:"About 40cm from head to tail. Lives from Canada to central California. Cat-sized with fluffy fur. Males are black all over, with 2 beautiful thick white stripes running from the back of the head down both sides of the back. Females are a bit smaller and brownish. Its weapon is a “super-smelly yellow liquid” from its rear; it's not a fart. The smell is said to be hundreds of times worse than eggs, meat and fish mixed and left rotting for days. Even bathing daily, it won't come off for a week, and if it gets on your clothes, no washing helps; you have to throw them away. Best not to mess with one.",
61:"Tanuki",
62:"Body about 70cm. In Japan it lives on low mountains everywhere. Found only in East Asia, it has a cross-shaped black patch on its back and is also called “cross mujina.” It lives in rock dens, eats anything and often climbs trees. Its pelt is used against the cold and its hair makes fine brushes, but it doesn't taste very good. It's easily confused with the badger, but tanuki are dogs and badgers are weasels.",
63:"Gray Squirrel",
64:"Body 43-50cm, tail 21-24cm, weight about 400-710g. Lives in the eastern half of the U.S. It's often seen chattering “kik-kik” and flicking its tail in the morning and evening. It strips bark off sycamore, beech and oak trees, which is a problem.",
65:"Nile Crocodile",
66:"About 6m from head to tail. Lives in Africa. It flees into the water when approached, but it's fierce and a dangerous croc that may attack people. A bird called the Egyptian plover cleans its teeth, but as nature is destroyed their numbers fall, and you can hardly ever see the cleaning anymore.",
67:"Japanese Hare",
68:"Body about 45-60cm. Lives in Honshu, Shikoku and Kyushu. In winter it turns white except for its black ear tips.",
69:"Jungle Crow",
70:"Body about 55-60cm. Widespread from Japan to East and South Asia. Its feathers are black with a blue-green sheen, and its wings shine purple. It has a thick beak and eats anything.",
71:"Prairie Dog",
72:"Body 29-32cm, tail 6-7cm, weight 900-1,300g. Lives in families in underground burrows on North American grasslands. A lookout stands guard by the burrow and warns the others with a dog-like bark when danger nears. As the name says it lives on the prairie, but it's not a dog; it's a squirrel. Wild ones live about 2-4 years, pets 7-8 years and zoo ones about 6-12.",
73:"Moose",
74:"Body 2.4-2.9m, 1.5-1.9m at the shoulder, weight about 500-800kg. Lives in cold northern places like Scandinavia, Russia and North America. Called moose in English, it's bigger than a horse and the largest deer. Males have splendid flat, palm-like antlers.",
75:"Arctic Fox",
76:"Body 46-68cm, tail 26-43cm, weight about 2.5-9kg. Lives in tundra and coasts from northern Eurasia and northeast North America to Greenland. Its ears are small and round. There are 2 types: the blue fox, dark brown with a blue tint all year, and the white fox, pure white in winter and grayish brown in summer.",
77:"Polar Bear",
78:"A big bear reaching 2.2m. Lives around the Arctic. Its long neck sets it apart from other bears, and it's creamy white all over except for its black nose and claws. It lives alone, swims well and eats seals, fish and even seaweed.",
79:"Red Fox",
80:"Body 62-73.5cm, tail 34-39cm, weight about 3-7kg. Lives in Honshu, Shikoku, Kyushu and so on. The red fox outside Hokkaido, it sometimes attacks chickens. The one in Hokkaido is the “Ezo red fox.”",
81:"Flying Squirrel",
82:"Body about 15-20cm. Lives in mountain forests from Siberia to Japan. It glides from tree to tree （about 8m） and eats bugs and nuts. People once didn't tell it apart from the giant flying squirrel, but it's smaller, with a long tail flattened top to bottom.",
83:"Wild Boar",
84:"Lives in mountains of North Africa and Japan's Honshu, Shikoku and Kyushu. The ancestor of pigs, it's fierce and raises the hair along its back when angry. Babies have stripes and are called “uribo.” It tastes good; long called “mountain whale,” it's eaten in hot pots and such.",
}
